Abstract = {Immune-stromal crosstalk governs tissue fibrosis, which is marked by
   dysregulated extracellular matrix (ECM) production and aberrant
   vasculature. Here, we investigate how gamma delta T cell interactions
   with stromal cells shape fibrosis in the foreign body response in a
   murine biomaterial implant model. During the acute reaction, type-1
   (gamma delta IFN gamma) and type-17 (gamma delta 17) gamma delta T cell
   subsets accumulate at the implant site. While gamma delta IFN gamma
   cells decrease as fibrosis progresses, activated gamma delta 17 cells
   persist as dominant interleukin-17 producers. The gamma delta 17 cell
   compartment expands with aging and high-fat diet, both factors
   associated with chronic inflammation and fibrosis. Fibroblasts
   co-cultured with gamma delta 17 cells exhibit increased expression of
   collagen genes and intercellular communication inference links gamma
   delta T cell ligands to activation of ECM remodeling and vascular
   development programs in fibroblasts and endothelial cells. Finally,
   genetic deletion of gamma delta T cells in mice alters expression of ECM
   components and increases vessel size within the fibrotic matrix.
   Altogether, our findings implicate gamma delta T cells in regulating
   stromal cell behavior to modulate composition and vascularity of
   fibrotic tissues.},
